<Project>

  <!--
    Applies to every project in the solution: MSBuild walks up from each .csproj until it finds
    this file, so SmartX.Api, SmartX.Client and SmartX.Shared all pick it up.

    THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES

    Downloading this repository as a ZIP from GitHub and extracting it with Explorer nests the
    folder inside itself:

        Downloads\...-andrefourie12-main\...-andrefourie12-main\SmartX\SmartX.Client\

    That is 163 characters before the build writes anything. WPF's XAML compilation then spins
    up a temporary project and writes intermediate files such as

        SmartX.Client_s3k4a1vj_wpftmp.GeneratedMSBuildEditorConfig.editorconfig

    which is 71 characters of filename on its own. Add the obj\Debug\net10.0-windows\ folders
    and the path reaches 261 - past Windows' 260-character MAX_PATH - and the build fails with
    "Could not find a part of the path ...". `dotnet build` survives it because the .NET SDK's
    MSBuild is long-path aware; Visual Studio's MSBuild.exe is not, so it looks like an IDE bug.

    WHY THE INTERMEDIATES HAVE TO LEAVE THE FOLDER

    Shortening obj\ inside the project cannot fix it. The markup-compile pass hands its own
    IntermediateOutputPath to the temporary project, so "Debug\net10.0-windows\" comes back
    whatever this file says. 163 + 22 forced + 71 filename leaves nothing to trim.

    So when - and only when - the project sits too deep to build safely, the intermediates go to
    a short fixed root instead, where the path length depends on the user's account name rather
    than on how deeply the repository was extracted. That brings the same file down to about 160
    characters.

    The 140-character threshold: a project directory can afford
    "\obj\Debug\net10.0-windows\" (27) plus a 71-character filename and still stay under 260, so
    anything up to about 161 is safe. 140 leaves room to spare. A normal clone is far below it
    and keeps the ordinary obj\ folder beside the project, exactly as before - this only changes
    behaviour for checkouts that would otherwise fail outright.

    Only obj moves. bin\ is untouched, so the built .exe stays where you expect it.

    The hash of the project directory stops two checkouts of this repository from sharing one
    intermediate folder and overwriting each other's build state.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' and '$(LOCALAPPDATA)' != '' and $(MSBuildProjectDirectory.Length) &gt; 140">
    <BaseIntermediateOutputPath>$(LOCALAPPDATA)\SmartX-build\$([MSBuild]::StableStringHash($(MSBuildProjectDirectory)))\$(MSBuildProjectName)\</BaseIntermediateOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Required whenever obj\ is redirected above. The SDK normally excludes the intermediate
      folder from compilation by deriving the exclusion from BaseIntermediateOutputPath; once
      that points elsewhere, any obj\ folder left behind by an earlier build stops being
      excluded, and the .cs files the SDK generated in it (AssemblyInfo.cs and friends) get
      compiled a second time - "error CS0579: Duplicate ... attribute".

      Stated explicitly, so a stale obj\ folder is ignored in either mode and switching between
      them never needs a manual clean.
    -->
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);obj\**;bin\**</DefaultItemExcludes>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
